It's not, uh, it's not like the same look everywhere you go.

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:10.540
Yes. But it's still like contextualized through this, this land kind of.

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:13.460
Yeah. It felt to me like, you know, it's like, oh, okay.

00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:18.900
There's, you got middle earth and you got, you know, you got these other like fantasy worlds, you know, you got Narnia, you got everything.

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:21.080
And this fantasy world is a tree.

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:22.620
And I just thought that was cool.

00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:30.740
Like, that was just interesting. It's not a land, the whole city, the whole whatever, not city, but the whole country or the whole nation or whatever it is, is a tree.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:32.500
And that was just interesting to me.

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:36.120
It sounds really dumb when I say it too many times.

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:38.900
Tree's a funny word.

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:45.820
Tree's a funny word.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:13.320
The, the, the Jack key is available to you and they just have like, it's just key with a J symbol.

00:21:13.480 --> 00:21:17.340
And then you get a key with a Q symbol later for queen and stuff like that.

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:21.080
Um, you have to buy these keys in order to leave the town.

00:21:21.400 --> 00:21:28.020
And then you also have to stock up on them in general because you don't know when you're going to need that key of Jack next for a different location.

00:21:29.540 --> 00:21:37.980
And I don't know, like we're, we've already seen like a lot of implementations of keys in dungeon based video games, handling it a lot better.

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:44.980
So I'm not quite sure why they made the decision to have like a key shop where you have to buy and stock up on keys.

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:57.040
And that every time you leave a town like, or an area that requires a key, you have to have that key ready again in order to reenter those areas.

00:21:57.040 --> 00:22:08.480
It seems like something that could have easily have been solved even with NES tech to just like, once you find this key, whether it's talking to somebody, completing an objective, finding it in a dungeon.

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:16.640
Once you, once you've found the correct key and can then unlock that door, then, you know, that door stays permanently unlocked.

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:24.940
Of course, the problem with that kind of thing is that you run into, uh, you know, that like, it's that specific key for that specific thing.

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:26.600
And so you're gated from everything else.

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:37.680
Having the key of Jack be like specific for some doors, but the key of Queen for others, like having that be the system allows for, I guess, more experimentation and going to areas that maybe you necessarily shouldn't have been.

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:43.560
Which is great, but I can't help but feel like you could have also then just made the keys that you find incredibly generic.

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:59.820
And once you find them, it's your decision, which door you want to open with similar to what Legend of Zelda did with the dungeons where it's like you, you know, there's enough keys in there to go through every room, but you also don't need to go into every room of the dungeon to do everything that you need to do in the game.

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:11.760
Well, one thing that I didn't really get about this mechanic is like, it's one thing to have this key seller and have to like sort of go back to the town.

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:18.020
Once you find a door that's locked in like, okay, I got to get the J key or the K key or whatever the hell.

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:23.240
But what I didn't understand is why they're also cheap.

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:32.880
Like you can find the gold necessary to get as many keys as you need pretty easily so that all it really does is make this like a frustration feature.

00:23:33.680 --> 00:23:39.220
Like, like, like, oh, I have to go back to get the key that costs $100 or 100 golds.

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:46.260
Sorry. Uh, 100 golds to, to get it, to get a J key so that now I have to backtrack to get this.

00:23:46.580 --> 00:24:02.360
I, it's either like it would have made more sense for it to be like, you need to have like, like a certain amount of experience to get through this key or you find the keys throughout the levels instead of this kind of.

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:07.100
Almost useless, uh, act of buying them because again, they're so cheap.

00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:10.320
I don't know what you guys thought about that.

00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:24.780
I say, well, so before I, before I, you know, um, jump into that, just the, the problem that I had with the keys specifically, um, was, was the fact that the, that the doors don't remain unlocked.

00:24:24.860 --> 00:24:27.900
That was really annoying because there is backtracking involved in this game.

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:34.100
So like, I felt like I shouldn't have to buy another key and, and take up another slot in my inventory to open a door I already opened.

00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:44.480
Um, as far as the, like having to backtrack and like having these, these keys that aren't that expensive, I think it was in some senses a good, in some senses a bad thing.

00:24:44.480 --> 00:24:54.360
I, like, I actually, I think that there were times where that, that enhanced a little bit the exploration of it where I ran into a door and like, oh, there's a Q symbol on it.

00:24:54.420 --> 00:24:55.680
I haven't run into a Q symbol yet.

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:57.760
I haven't had access to the queen keys yet.

00:24:58.140 --> 00:24:59.320
I don't know what that is.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.580
And, and I'm making that, maybe it's not the queen, but with, with, with one of the key types, this has happened.

00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:09.180
And, and then later on I find a shop that sells these and I'm like, oh, I wonder what's in that door.

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:10.900
Now I can go back and check out that door.

00:25:11.180 --> 00:25:16.940
And, and there are other times where it's, you know, it is just like part of my exploration is like, I came across this door.

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:17.900
I can't open it.

00:25:18.080 --> 00:25:20.080
I'll keep going and I'll make a mental note.

00:25:20.280 --> 00:25:21.560
I should, I should pick up a key.

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:24.460
It's just that little bit of immersion of like going to the town.

00:25:24.460 --> 00:25:29.720
I mean, I would have been more frustrated if the keys weren't as cheap as they were.

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:41.620
But like, I think it's like not a perfect system in general, but, but it didn't, it didn't feel like a really negative thing to me other than the fact that the doors don't remain unlocked.

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:46.540
Granted with that said, where I left off, I, I, I got pretty far in the game.

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:53.160
I think I'm only like one or two like stages from the end and, and where I sort of stopped.

00:25:53.980 --> 00:25:59.540
Uh, at least for now is somewhere where I got kind of stuck where I need keys that I don't have.

00:25:59.620 --> 00:26:00.720
And I have to backtrack to get the keys.

00:26:00.820 --> 00:26:06.860
So I guess that, that, that ended up being kind of an annoyance to me, but for the majority of this game, it didn't feel like an annoyance.

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:11.660
It just felt like another thing that like makes me feel immersed in like stock up on potions.

00:26:11.660 --> 00:26:14.280
Like, Hey, were there any keys that I remember seeing that I need to grab?

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:15.460
Let's grab one of those.

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:22.560
Or if I feel comfortable enough, not having my inventory full of potions, maybe I'll, maybe I'll take a, take a bet that like,

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:24.460
Oh, I'll probably need a J key again at some point.

00:26:24.580 --> 00:26:25.580
So maybe I'll grab one now.

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:31.300
Yeah. And you're not, you're never that far away from where you need to go to get keys.

00:26:31.960 --> 00:26:39.140
So again, it's not, it's not a, a huge problem, but I did find it to be that kind of annoyance.

00:26:40.060 --> 00:26:54.220
And Joe, you reminded me that the inventory system in the game limits the amount of things that you're able to hold, I think to seven items, not in that counting like your armor and weapons and stuff, but just like in your potions or keys and stuff like that.

00:26:54.220 --> 00:26:56.560
And that is, that is okay with me.

00:26:56.660 --> 00:27:05.260
Like I'm fine with, you know, being like, okay, well you can only have so many life-saving potions that you can buy again, pretty cheaply, just like the, uh, the keys.

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:12.200
Uh, but that, you know, you, you can't have more than seven on your adventure through a dungeon, which to be honest is plenty enough, you know?

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:17.000
But I still don't think that that's an argument for like the keys being part of that management.

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:21.600
Like keys can just be something next to experience and gold in the UI screen.

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:33.060
Like, I'm not sure, uh, that needs to be something that you, you know, you actively manage in terms of like, oh, gotta go back to town and buy more keys because they keep entering and exiting this dungeon.

00:27:33.440 --> 00:27:36.040
Yeah. I mean, I, it's an interesting way to do it.

00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:40.220
I think that the fact that you have to keep entering and exiting the same dungeon that needs the key.

00:27:40.220 --> 00:27:42.500
Yeah. That is exactly what I think the problem is with it.

00:27:42.600 --> 00:27:43.800
So it's like, yeah, you got to keep going back.

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:48.680
Yeah. The management thing, like, I don't think it's the best way, but I just think it's a way to do it.

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:53.900
I don't think it's like a, I don't think I didn't find it as much of a negative if you were to take out the other negatives.

00:27:54.720 --> 00:28:02.060
No, I agree. And we're probably, uh, we're probably rolling on a little too long because we haven't even talked about the combat yet, which I guess is the, the next step.

00:28:02.420 --> 00:28:06.700
If we're following, like, you've been in the town, you visited the shops, you bought the keys.

00:28:06.700 --> 00:28:14.300
Now you're out in the, uh, the world of the tree and you're going to run into enemies and you're going to have to deal with those enemies with combat.

00:28:14.660 --> 00:28:21.960
Uh, very similar to adventure of link with, you know, you have your sword, you have your shield and, uh, you have magic that you can use too.

00:28:22.820 --> 00:28:29.240
What did you guys think of, you know, don't, don't pretend like you're, uh, you're fully equipped, right?

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:31.420
With all the magic you have in the entire game or anything.

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:33.180
So like just, just when you're starting out, right.

00:28:33.260 --> 00:28:38.040
And all you really have is your sword and, um, the initial, I think it's like a fireball, right?

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:41.820
It's called deluge, but yeah, it's just a projectile.

00:28:42.160 --> 00:28:45.500
Great. Okay. So, and you just have that, you know, and that's how you start out this game.

00:28:45.680 --> 00:28:47.980
How does combat feel like right from the beginning?

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:50.480
It's not, it's not crazy bad.

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:56.700
It's also a kind of sticky in some cases, but it, and you know what?

00:28:56.700 --> 00:28:58.960
I think I, I misrepresented it already.

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:00.760
I think it's perfectly fine.

00:29:01.160 --> 00:29:16.760
Um, the, uh, the enemies are designed in a way that I think the, the, um, the skill that you will develop when you, um, as you continue to fight these guys.

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:19.580
Is your spacing.

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:25.640
Like you're going to want to make sure that you are a certain distance away from these guys.

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:39.500
Uh, because whether they're jumping or they're firing projectiles, like you, there's a lot of timing that comes with, um, uh, their patterns and you trying to move forward at the same time and not trying to take damage.

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:41.580
That said, you do get a lot of health.

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:52.000
Um, I'd say the controls were a little sticky, um, and there's knockback, which can also be a little frustrating, but that's, that's part of it.

00:29:52.580 --> 00:30:01.520
Um, I I'd say that it, it definitely feels, um, not like a cookie cutter from any other game.

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:04.760
Um, but it's not doing anything too crazy either.

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:12.920
Yeah. I mean, it, it feels, it feels like some, a little bit like, like a variation of different things that we've seen before.

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:23.520
Um, and I did, especially at first notice a little bit of that stiffness, that clunkiness and it, and it got me hit and, and dead quite a bit, um, early on.

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:30.880
But, but I'll say that it is, it is like something that you get used to very quickly.

00:30:31.640 --> 00:30:41.380
And, and I feel like by the, you know, by the time I was like, you know, an hour or two into this game, I had gotten pretty good at, at understanding the movement of this game.

00:30:41.460 --> 00:30:49.340
Cause like at first, especially like even just the jumping, if you're in combat, you're trying to jump over something or even just the platforming is not, is, is very stiff.

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:52.900
And like the, your attack is stiff and, and there's that knockback.

00:30:53.220 --> 00:31:02.300
But after a while, like I got really used to it and I got, I feel like it felt really good after a while to actually, to actually engage in combat in this.

00:31:02.440 --> 00:31:10.900
And that knockback that you have, the enemies have too, which sounds like a given, but there's so many games that we've had where that just doesn't exist or it's so minimal.

00:31:11.060 --> 00:31:16.420
But like, I really feel like I'm like wording this, this enemy back when I, you know, you hit it once, it gets some knockback.

00:31:16.420 --> 00:31:17.780
You can kind of gain ground on it.

00:31:17.900 --> 00:31:19.780
If it hits you, it gains ground on you.

00:31:20.020 --> 00:31:21.920
Every battle feels a little bit like that.

00:31:22.100 --> 00:31:30.180
And, and there are different, you know, magic even sometimes affects it differently, not just in like how it hurts them, but how it knocks them back.

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:35.800
Which like, once you start getting different magic and stuff, makes it, it makes it interesting.

00:31:35.920 --> 00:31:43.400
And it, it's, it's, it's just, I guess I'm just trying to say it was weird how much of a different opinion I had when I started this game,

00:31:43.400 --> 00:31:48.080
as opposed to even when I was a couple hours into the game as to like how good it felt.

00:31:48.260 --> 00:31:56.760
Cause at first I was kind of like, okay, like this is, this is good, but it's not, it's, it's, it's got a little, some of those problems I've, I've had with it in the past.

00:31:56.900 --> 00:32:04.400
But after a while, I mean, I played this, um, I played this actually like pretty straight through in like one, one long sitting.

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:08.620
Uh, I played for like nine hours because I just didn't have a lot of time to play before this.

00:32:08.780 --> 00:32:15.420
So I played like nine hours straight and like by the, by like, by hour two, I was like, I am loving this combat system.

00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:22.800
I was feeling, it was feeling like I had like a flow feels like the wrong word because it is sort of stiff, but I just, I just felt like I really had a handle on it.

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:31.860
And I think that that means that there's, that there's some, something going on in the, in the way that the game moves that that's working.

00:32:32.060 --> 00:32:43.940
I guess I never really, uh, thought about it in the context of, or I'd never really, it never really registered with me that I was inflicting knockback with my, um, attacks as well.

00:32:43.940 --> 00:32:55.120
Because I guess that's why I, I was sort of describing the combat as more like spacing base, kind of like fencing, uh, even though not all the enemies have like a weapon.

00:32:55.300 --> 00:33:07.540
Some of them are just like jumping at you, but that is sort of what you're managing throughout the whole game is just like the distance between you and the enemy, um, at any given time, just to like get that, that perfect hit in.

00:33:07.540 --> 00:33:10.860
Um, and then that knockback is sort of your reward.

00:33:11.140 --> 00:33:18.220
Um, even though, uh, this might be just something that I was feeling, but that that's sort of how I was, I was thinking about it.

00:33:18.720 --> 00:33:27.140
Yeah, actually, similarly, I hadn't thought of it that way, but as you're saying that, that's exactly how I was feeling was like, I can tell where like this enemy is going to end up.

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:31.740
And if I, if I do it poorly, he's going to hit me, but if I get myself in the right spot, I'll be able to hit him.

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:41.280
Um, the spacing comment is, uh, the one that rings most true for me, Sean, because, uh, you know, you saying fencing, I think we said that about Zelda 2 as well.

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:57.740
What I really like about the way the game handles the spacing of even just its enemies and you is that you're never really overloaded with enemies, but also the way enemies behave in this game, uh, isn't something where like, you know, uh, in Castlevania,

00:33:57.740 --> 00:34:05.920
uh, there are enemies like those little, um, those little gremlin characters, you know, that hop like really radically and like all over the place.

00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:16.400
And they, uh, you know, like they're, they're just a nuisance to deal with because the weapons that you have and the way that they move and the size of them on the screen, it doesn't like correlate to a fun experience.

00:34:16.400 --> 00:34:19.080
It's more like a, I hate when that enemy shows up.

00:34:19.200 --> 00:34:25.980
There's really no character in the enemy in this game that feels like that where you're like, oh, I can't believe I'm dealing with these guys.

00:34:25.980 --> 00:34:32.200
Like they, they included some annoying characters, like those bumblebees and stuff like that, but they're not like, they're never cheap.

00:34:32.300 --> 00:34:45.440
And the amount of characters on screen and the amount of like space in a, in a particular like screen that you're on with the amount of enemies they provide with you makes it, uh, you know, a fun challenge of like,

00:34:45.500 --> 00:34:52.220
how am I going to deal with the, the platforming and the way that the enemies are laid out?

00:34:52.220 --> 00:34:57.940
Because, you know, you could be dealing with like two, uh, two different enemies, right?

00:34:58.140 --> 00:35:01.660
But there's, because it's always, it's never on like flat ground, right?

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:06.300
There's always like either ladders that you can climb or, you know, different platforms to jump onto.

00:35:06.680 --> 00:35:10.620
There's like a way to think about how to take on the enemies on screen.

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:16.560
And yeah, they'll regenerate if you go back to the next screen, which is good for, you know, farming experience or collecting gold.

00:35:16.960 --> 00:35:20.940
There's never, no, no two screens are the same either.

00:35:20.940 --> 00:35:24.260
So you're always running into a different way of dealing with encounters.

00:35:24.260 --> 00:35:32.820
So combat never gets stale in, in that, in that sense, you know, the only time combat gets stale is if you were up to the task of grinding,

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:37.360
which I don't think the game necessarily forces on you, but you can get stuff earlier by doing it as such.

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:43.960
But I really just appreciate the types of enemies that they gave you while increasing the challenge as the game went on too,

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:47.320
but never giving you anything that felt cheap or unfair.

00:35:47.720 --> 00:35:51.920
The one thing, and I think this is a me thing, uh, cause I've said this with multiple games,

00:35:52.060 --> 00:35:58.080
is I still had problems with like the hitbox detection where like sometimes my sword would just be like,

00:35:58.260 --> 00:36:00.700
I feel like I would poke it at their nose, you know?

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:04.500
Like it wasn't enough to do any critical damage, but I was like, I'm just right there.

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:12.200
Yeah. I, yeah, I never had it with me inflicting damage or maybe I didn't.

00:36:12.260 --> 00:36:13.580
I just wasn't really noticing.

00:36:13.660 --> 00:36:20.220
I had it more when I was trying to avoid damage, like jumping over, uh, enemies.

00:36:20.360 --> 00:36:22.260
I'm like, I definitely cleared that guy.

00:36:22.260 --> 00:36:30.260
But like when you're jumping over someone and you get hit, you kind of get teleported back to the ground and you just have to live with yourself.

00:36:30.260 --> 00:36:32.880
It's like, that is very punishing.

00:36:33.180 --> 00:36:35.820
Just that feeling of getting slammed back down to earth.

00:36:35.940 --> 00:36:41.000
Cause in a lot of games, like if you, if you're jumping, you take damage, like it gives you feedback.

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:45.880
You either flash or your, your jump gets disturbed in some way or like, it's not the same arc.

00:36:45.920 --> 00:36:52.520
But like when you get hit, uh, when you just like clip the guy's hitbox by a, by a pixel and you get slammed back down,

00:36:52.620 --> 00:36:53.800
it's just like, Oh my God.

00:36:53.800 --> 00:36:56.260
So yeah, it wasn't offensive for me.

00:36:56.400 --> 00:36:59.520
It was more that defensive big hitbox.

00:36:59.760 --> 00:37:06.460
And another interesting thing about that knockback mechanic is, you know, we've seen that in other games, uh,

00:37:06.640 --> 00:37:16.000
where if you get hit by an enemy on a platforming like stage, and then you get knocked by the enemy so bad that you fall into the pit and die.

00:37:16.000 --> 00:37:18.740
You know, that's like, that's a NES gamers worst nightmare.

00:37:18.740 --> 00:37:21.280
Cause it happens so often in this game.

00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:28.560
Yes. You can fall into like holes that knock you down to a, um, lower part of the dungeon, right.

00:37:28.700 --> 00:37:33.740
Or the town that you're exploring, not town, but like the overworld area that you're exploring to get to the next town.

00:37:33.840 --> 00:37:38.120
Like that can happen, but there are no actual pits in this game.

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:44.700
So it's never a major consequence, but it is, you know, there is some punishment involved with, with what's happening.

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:53.800
If you happen to fall through these things now, granted, they've also turned that into puzzles sometimes where you want to fall through a pit to get to an area that you couldn't have got to otherwise.

00:37:53.800 --> 00:37:58.280
But I think like, you know, that's an interesting trade-off for knockback, right.

00:37:58.420 --> 00:38:11.060
Is like, it adds an element of feeling to, to being hit, but it also like does something in progression wise that we haven't seen with most games and that there is no game over or fall damage taken.

00:38:11.280 --> 00:38:14.220
It's just, you know, a, uh, a progression setback.

00:38:14.880 --> 00:38:25.120
Yes, exactly. And I, I think that that's a lot, uh, a lot easier to deal with, um, than, and a lot less frustrating than a one hit death.

00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:27.860
Yeah, definitely.

00:38:28.220 --> 00:38:40.560
And I think that if this, if this game did have those like, I don't know, stage hazards or, or like pits or, or even like spikes or something like that, and you kept getting knocked back, like there was this amount of knockback into those kinds of things.

00:38:40.560 --> 00:38:45.140
That would be a frustrating and, and, and, and sort of unfair feeling experience.

00:38:45.140 --> 00:38:50.480
But because of, because of the layout of these games, I think that the knockback, um, yeah, it doesn't give you those problems.

00:38:51.340 --> 00:38:53.780
This is sort of like a forgiving game in general.

00:38:53.900 --> 00:38:58.480
Like it never, like you're given a lot of health, a lot of mana.

00:38:58.980 --> 00:39:01.680
Uh, you can't fall down a hole.

00:39:02.240 --> 00:39:07.000
Um, uh, you're only really ever losing a little bit of progress at a time.

00:39:07.520 --> 00:39:09.580
Uh, did you guys feel the same way?

00:39:09.580 --> 00:39:11.460
Is this, is this an easier game?

00:39:12.380 --> 00:39:13.300
I think so.

00:39:13.400 --> 00:39:21.200
Because it also, you have to remember when you die, you just go back to wherever you last saved with, uh, the meditation stuff.

00:39:21.400 --> 00:39:29.760
Uh, those, yeah, those guys are the gurus and, um, you, the potions restore you back to full health.

00:39:29.960 --> 00:39:39.040
So like, there's a lot of, a lot of forgiveness, but I think it helps in these earlier NES games where they're trying to tell bigger stories, right?

00:39:39.040 --> 00:39:45.800
This isn't the same thing as, you know, level one, level two, and like seeing how far you can go before you die, right?

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:47.440
It's a, it's a totally different kind of game.

00:39:47.700 --> 00:39:56.480
And so I kind of appreciate it just like how we appreciated Dragon Warrior, uh, you know, being able to just save by, uh, talking to the king.

00:39:56.480 --> 00:39:59.440
And like, if you die, it's not like, well, when did you last save?

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:01.020
Because that's where you're going back to.

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:02.440
Like, you just lost some gold.

00:40:02.620 --> 00:40:06.760
I feel like that's the appropriate level of punishment in these older games.

00:40:06.760 --> 00:40:13.340
The whole, like, reset back to the beginning of a game or even like back to where you last saved.

00:40:13.900 --> 00:40:23.080
Well, maybe not where you last saved, but, you know, definitely when, when it's game over, I'm going to view those games a little harsher now that we have had games that have been a little more forgiving.

00:40:23.080 --> 00:40:32.920
Because as history shows, forgiveness in, um, you know, skill level is something that is appreciated by almost all video games now.

00:40:33.620 --> 00:40:39.960
Yeah. And I think that this game being, being easier, and for the record, I don't think this game is easy.

00:40:40.240 --> 00:40:42.040
I think it's just easier than a lot of games.

00:40:42.140 --> 00:40:51.460
And I think that makes it like much more accessible than some games where it's like, I don't have to get really good at this one specific, really Nintendo hard game.

00:40:51.460 --> 00:40:55.340
And maybe that's just like the 90s kid who didn't grow up with these games in me.

00:40:56.420 --> 00:41:04.660
But, but like, this felt much more accessible than a lot of other games we've played that were right out of the bat a lot harder and a lot less forgiving.

00:41:05.060 --> 00:41:13.420
But like, I don't think this game is like, oh, you know, here's a, you know, here's, everyone's happy and here's a really easy, like, walk in the park for you.

00:41:13.420 --> 00:41:23.740
But it, but it was like, not like, okay, you have to, you have to practice, you have to actually practice this game before you could really start going anywhere in it, which I feel like a lot of other games have been.

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:37.300
I'll even say that I do think that this is, because it's, I think like the combat is a little easier than combat in Zelda 2, that I think that I could see the argument that this is a little more accessible than essential game Zelda 2.

00:41:37.300 --> 00:41:42.180
Well, if you remember Zelda 2, though, there was like two different types of combat.

00:41:42.380 --> 00:41:48.200
There was the random encounters on the, and they weren't random, you walked into them, but they like spawned randomly.

00:41:49.280 --> 00:41:51.020
You know, encounters in the overworld.

00:41:51.160 --> 00:41:54.160
And then there was the dungeon style combat.

00:41:54.160 --> 00:42:08.400
Now, this whole game takes the dungeon style combat where everything is also like in, you know, involved in progression and the layout of the particular dungeon you're in, which is arguably the best part of Zelda 2.

00:42:08.400 --> 00:42:26.020
Because when you're running into the more random encounters just to grind up for experience and such, they just put like, either it's completely flat land or they put like, you know, some, some blocks to, to add a, a place to, to have to jump over or for enemies to be unable to reach you.

00:42:26.020 --> 00:42:29.060
They just place those things throughout, depending on the location you're in.

00:42:29.180 --> 00:42:30.580
And it doesn't feel as purposeful.

00:42:30.700 --> 00:42:45.500
Whereas this whole game, like every screen does feel like they at least thought, oh, well, we should put an enemy here because just even based on the simple coding we did where enemies always just, you know, they're like heat seeking missiles.

00:42:45.660 --> 00:42:47.960
They have to go towards the direction that you're in.

00:42:48.040 --> 00:42:51.920
Not even if it's makes sense for them, but just that's how they behave.

00:42:51.920 --> 00:42:58.560
They at least placed enemies in ways that made sense then based on like which screen you might enter in first or, or such.

00:42:58.720 --> 00:43:11.160
So I do think even if the combat is easier because, you know, you have, it's more forgiving in terms of amounts of hits you can take and, um, and the amount of enemies that they put on a screen.

00:43:12.080 --> 00:43:19.820
It's still thought about, like, it's not just like, oh, it's easy because there's only, you know, this one enemy, one or two enemies on every single screen.

00:43:19.820 --> 00:43:35.320
It's just more thought out and more like the whole idea of the game is just to continue building experience, getting new armor, new weapons, new magic, and furthering this, uh, this quest that you're on to, um, to take down the evil one.

00:43:36.040 --> 00:43:37.020
Yeah, absolutely.

00:43:37.280 --> 00:43:42.940
I think this, this whole game feels like, um, yeah, like you're getting stronger as a warrior and taking out these monsters.

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:47.880
So, so it takes a lot to kill you after a while, after you get good at this game.

00:43:47.880 --> 00:43:50.320
And, you know, some enemies do way more damage than others.

00:43:50.660 --> 00:43:52.240
Some are like actually devastating if you get hit.

00:43:52.640 --> 00:44:01.300
But for the most part, like, it feels like you're this warrior and like what's going to kill you is being out in this dangerous area for too long or getting hit by too many enemies over time.

00:44:01.860 --> 00:44:15.740
And, and, and whereas Zelda 2 felt a little more like sometimes I would, even in those side-scrolling dungeon levels, I would get into like, I would find an enemy and it would be like each of the, like each encounter I kind of got into felt a little like a one-on-one duel.

00:44:15.740 --> 00:44:23.820
Like, this guy is almost as powerful as me and like, I have to really think about like, how am I going to get past his shield before he gets past my shield and, and, or something like that.

00:44:23.920 --> 00:44:31.480
Like I have to jump and like avoid him more as if he's like a one-on-one enemy, which I, I think they're both interesting ways to go about it in their own right.

00:44:31.640 --> 00:44:33.860
But like, they just make the games feel, feel different.

00:44:46.900 --> 00:45:03.280
We were giving our impressions of the combat system and I think we went pretty in depth, but just to continue that conversation with the, the magic system and, and the progression of combat in general, like, you know, you're given access to pretty much everything that you will do in the game at the start of the game.

00:45:03.280 --> 00:45:09.680
But then it evolves in terms of the, the magic you have, the weapons you have, the armories that you have and stuff like that.

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:14.560
Does the combat actually feel, we mentioned that you're feeling stronger, sure.

00:45:14.560 --> 00:45:22.120
But does the combat actually feel like it changes based on the magic system and what you learn throughout the game?

00:45:23.720 --> 00:45:24.620
I think so.

00:45:24.780 --> 00:45:29.900
Yeah. I think that it's, it, one, because of the enemy variety.

00:45:30.140 --> 00:45:41.100
I mean, there, like I said, I'm really, I'm, I'm many hours into this game and I'm still getting introduced to new enemies that do new things, which, which changes things up.

00:45:41.100 --> 00:45:49.000
And then also like, yeah, the weapons are, you know, are like usually just like a longer version of your last weapon that does more damage for the most part.

00:45:49.220 --> 00:45:51.320
But like, you know, that does make you think about things differently.

00:45:51.320 --> 00:45:55.800
Like you get, you get weapons that can hurt enemies that you couldn't hurt with a previous weapon at all.

00:45:56.700 --> 00:46:01.800
And the magic, some of the, I guess some of the magic affects things in different ways.

00:46:01.900 --> 00:46:11.200
I mean, you know, there's not a ton of magic variety, but you know, I know there's one spell that like knocks things to the back of the screen and is good for like crowd control or like giving yourself a second.

00:46:11.380 --> 00:46:13.120
And it actually became very useful.

00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:14.540
And apparently that's an optional spell.

00:46:15.060 --> 00:46:17.900
Um, so, or like an optional spell to even find it.

00:46:17.900 --> 00:46:18.920
I think they all are.

00:46:19.880 --> 00:46:25.540
So I guess they're all optional, but like some of, I think the one, the fire spell I'm talking about is not like, you don't find it in a town.

00:46:25.860 --> 00:46:33.520
It's like a, like a side area where there's a, where there's a, there's a hidden, not hidden, but like a, a missable shop.

00:46:33.660 --> 00:46:34.700
Old man with a beard.

00:46:35.320 --> 00:46:47.640
Yeah. Um, so for like, for that element, like, I do think that I, I felt like it was evolving, you know, it never got like crazy different than the beginning, but it felt like it was always, uh, the next.

00:46:47.640 --> 00:46:49.380
It was the first step in the progression of the combat.

00:46:50.260 --> 00:46:57.820
Yeah. And even, even little things like, and it's like, it's pretty early when you get the, the, the small shield.

00:46:58.380 --> 00:47:02.460
But when I was playing it without the shield, like everything hurt a lot.

00:47:02.660 --> 00:47:07.740
And then once you get the shield, you can kind of, you know, tank a little bit.

00:47:07.880 --> 00:47:15.620
You can sacrifice a hit or two, um, just to make the, uh, the rest of the combat a little more fluid.

00:47:15.620 --> 00:47:23.980
Like it's just tiny things like that, that it does make combat a bit more dynamic over the course of the game as you're leveling up.

00:47:24.220 --> 00:47:37.400
Yeah. And I think even the magic system in this game, you know, it's weird to compare it to dragon warrior, but we were kind of dunking on that because it didn't offer like a full experience of what we expect with an RPG.

00:47:37.960 --> 00:47:42.960
And, uh, even just, even just the names of the magic wasn't really satisfying enough.

00:47:42.960 --> 00:48:04.280
But in this game, you won't, you don't like get like a ton of different magic, but because it happens, you know, in this side scrolling action format, the magic does feel different enough that, uh, you know, whether you're doing, uh, the thunder projectile or the death projectile or whatever, like it feels like you are advancing.

00:48:05.800 --> 00:48:13.680
It's not like a ton of magic, right?

00:48:34.280 --> 00:48:40.140
Different looking projectile that basically does the same thing, but attributes more damage points to the enemy.

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:50.000
I guess that's also just a, um, uh, just a symptom of like, this is a game where you are one-to-one interacting with the environment.

00:48:50.260 --> 00:48:59.020
Whereas dragon, uh, dragon warrior is just, you decide to cast it and it happens and then you continue on.

00:48:59.220 --> 00:49:10.000
Uh, I guess, I don't know if I'm, if I'm saying that clearly enough, but since you're, you're on the ground with your, your character, instead of choosing from a menu,

00:49:10.000 --> 00:49:16.940
something to do and then moving on, it, it feels a little bit, uh, a little bit more satisfying to do.

00:49:17.160 --> 00:49:28.460
Yeah. And then they even make like the items that you can just like buy readily, like the wing boots or the hourglass or the, I think it's the matic, uh, the, the little pickaxe.

00:49:28.580 --> 00:49:37.800
Yeah. Um, you know, these items that you have, those almost feel like they could have been spells that just have different, you know, different uses than attacking enemies.

00:49:37.920 --> 00:49:51.440
Like it's, it's kind of interesting that they left magic to just be what I think of as final fantasy black magic stuff that like just does damage to characters rather than having any form of, you know, white magic where it's like things that affect the environment.

00:49:51.440 --> 00:49:55.300
Now, granted they do have those things in the form of items, but I wouldn't, I don't know.

00:49:55.380 --> 00:49:59.580
Why can't you have like a fly spell or a time spell where you stop?

00:49:59.900 --> 00:50:00.780
Like a TM?

00:50:01.160 --> 00:50:04.640
Yeah. Yeah. I was surprised that there wasn't even like a heel spell.

00:50:04.840 --> 00:50:09.920
I was, I knew, I was like at some point I knew from the beginning of the game at some point I'll get a heel spell, but no, there was no heel spell, which is fine.

00:50:09.980 --> 00:50:14.360
It's just not, you know, I think it just, it, uh, subverted my expectations.

00:50:14.680 --> 00:50:15.840
There's enough bread loaves.

00:50:16.160 --> 00:50:18.200
Yeah. That's true.

00:50:18.340 --> 00:50:19.600
In some areas. Yeah.

00:50:19.780 --> 00:50:24.520
And then, you know, there's enough red potions, I suppose that like you don't need to have that spell.

00:50:24.520 --> 00:50:33.500
I guess I was just surprised that they only thought of magic as a combat based thing and not as like a, you know, a puzzle solving mechanic throughout the dungeons.

00:50:33.620 --> 00:50:38.920
That would, that might've been interesting to, you know, lock something behind a certain magic that you need.

00:50:38.920 --> 00:50:58.620
Well, I guess in a way, and I know it is still an item, um, but like, yeah, the magic boots, like one of the first things that you have to do is to use the magic boots to, to fly up, um, and start of, and sort of like start the, uh, the fountain setting quest.

00:50:59.080 --> 00:51:00.980
Um, which I thought was pretty cool.

00:51:01.120 --> 00:51:11.400
And like, yeah, it is an item, but at least it's not just like, oh, I killed this boss that was, uh, that was protecting the, um, the fountain.

00:51:11.400 --> 00:51:21.040
It was actually something that you had to do out, not what, not quite outside the box, but it was a more unique interaction with the game to, to move forward.

00:51:21.320 --> 00:51:33.780
So whether it's through magic or an item, uh, it's still, I think a, uh, a more interesting way to go about it than just straight up like combat through everything.

00:51:39.620 --> 00:51:45.580
So forget about the enemies that you run into as you're exploring from town to town or going inside these dungeons.

00:51:45.960 --> 00:51:49.700
What is the exploration mechanic of this game?

00:51:49.860 --> 00:51:53.600
Like, is there, are there forks in the road here?

00:51:53.840 --> 00:51:55.940
Are there interesting paths that you can take?

00:51:55.940 --> 00:52:06.980
Like, or is it more just like a series of hallways that, uh, you know, that we see now is like loading screens in games, but like mostly just point A to point B to get to your next dungeon beat.

00:52:07.160 --> 00:52:10.440
Uh, which path do you guys feel like, uh, Fizanadu took?

00:52:10.760 --> 00:52:14.460
It seems like it starts out kind of open and then it narrows.

00:52:14.940 --> 00:52:18.120
Um, but that also could have just been my...

00:52:18.120 --> 00:52:19.340
Sure, and I think that's what we're interested in, right?

00:52:19.400 --> 00:52:21.660
Is like, you know, there's one way of looking at it, right?

00:52:21.660 --> 00:52:26.160
Where you could just like pull up the strategy guide and look at the maps and decide for yourself, right?

00:52:26.280 --> 00:52:36.200
But I think it's important to think about what your gameplay experience was like too, because you might have just continued to be like, well, the dungeons are the progression in the game.

00:52:36.200 --> 00:52:40.520
And for me, exploration is just based on, you know, moving along story-wise.

00:52:40.700 --> 00:52:44.340
So I don't need to like explore every nook and cranny of everything.

00:52:44.340 --> 00:52:47.700
I just want to get to the next dungeon, if you will.

00:52:47.700 --> 00:53:01.100
And I think that my, like, and this may just be, uh, like just rationalizing, but it almost makes sense in, uh, the context of the game where like you're at the base of a tree.

00:53:01.320 --> 00:53:02.860
It's a bit more spread out.

00:53:02.960 --> 00:53:06.880
You're going up a tree, uh, there's going to be some branches.

00:53:06.880 --> 00:53:11.380
And then like, once you're at like, you know, the top of the tree, like what you can't really explore anymore.

00:53:11.380 --> 00:53:13.540
You're just like at this little precipice.

00:53:13.640 --> 00:53:16.580
So I don't know that that's, that's one way to look at it.

00:53:17.300 --> 00:53:27.780
I, uh, so I, I thought that it was kind of, you know, it was linear in the sense that there, there are what, eight towns and you have to hit them in a certain order as you go.

00:53:28.000 --> 00:53:29.520
You're going to hit them along your way.

00:53:29.600 --> 00:53:33.140
And there's definitely like a linear path to the end.

00:53:33.140 --> 00:53:42.620
But I found there were a lot of, you know, like branching path, no, no pun intended, branching paths that like would take you to, to different areas.

00:53:42.640 --> 00:53:49.940
Maybe a lot is the wrong word, but, but more than I expected from a side scroller game like this, that felt like, like, oh, it could only really be linear.

00:53:49.940 --> 00:54:01.360
But, you know, you have that, it was the four paw or one of the first towns you go to where you have to do that, that fountain mission where there's, there's these three fountains and they're sort of scattered in different directions.

00:54:01.580 --> 00:54:03.200
And, you know, those are all things you have to do.

00:54:03.280 --> 00:54:05.180
It's kind of like a little detour before you move on.

00:54:05.180 --> 00:54:09.240
You have to explore this whole area of the tree, these different branches of the tree.

00:54:09.660 --> 00:54:22.060
Then later on, there's, um, when you're in like the mist area, uh, that you have like, you can get lost a little bit, but also like there are totally optional towers.

00:54:22.060 --> 00:54:34.620
I think in a couple of the areas between, you know, on the, a few of the different like in between areas between two towns, there are totally optional paths you can go on that'll take you to totally optional dungeons or towers or whatever you want to call them.

00:54:35.180 --> 00:54:45.700
Um, which I did one of them and got like some good, um, some good items and, and like there were, there, it felt like there was a reason to go to it.

00:54:47.140 --> 00:55:00.320
And I felt like each section had like a little bit of that, like, all right, to move on, I can go this way and to, you know, I could also explore this way and maybe find something good or maybe there's something interesting over here.

00:55:00.320 --> 00:55:05.740
Or like I said, there's a, there's a hidden house over here that sells a spell I can't get anywhere else in the game.

00:55:06.580 --> 00:55:16.640
Um, and I sort of balanced the like desire to just explore and see if I'm going the right direction or see what I find with also sometimes being like, okay, I want to go the right way.

00:55:16.920 --> 00:55:18.940
Sometimes I would look at a guide if I was lost.

00:55:18.940 --> 00:55:26.580
Um, but, but I did find more than I was expecting, I guess, of that like branching path stuff.

00:55:26.840 --> 00:55:29.620
Yeah, I guess I'd describe it more as like a hub and spoke.

00:55:30.140 --> 00:55:39.340
Um, uh, like these, each town like has, uh, some places that you can go to, but you're never really going to veer that far away.

00:55:39.340 --> 00:55:42.840
And then you can, you get back to the main path eventually.

00:55:43.440 --> 00:55:51.660
It's a lot less like insulting than a lot of, uh, more modern, like final fantasies where it is just sort of like a hallway simulator.

00:55:52.100 --> 00:55:54.480
Um, it actually has place.

00:55:54.640 --> 00:56:01.380
You have some latitude with how you can move, but you're always sort of going to find your way back to the, to the main drag.

00:56:01.500 --> 00:56:08.040
Yeah, and Joe, you were talking about how, you know, some, some optional towers and stuff like that, or even that you don't necessarily have to experience everything.

00:56:08.040 --> 00:56:12.640
And I, I thought that was a great implementation of even in the dungeons.

00:56:12.920 --> 00:56:15.740
Like there are so many rooms in the dungeons.

00:56:15.960 --> 00:56:28.220
It reminded me almost of, you know, the original Legend of Zelda there where, you know, you can, if you know the path to the boss, there are ways to get there significantly faster than maybe the, uh, most frequently traveled path.

00:56:28.220 --> 00:56:40.440
But with the dungeons in a game like this, you might think they just set it up so that, you know, do you just have to, you go into this screen and then there's a ladder and you go up that ladder and now you're on the next screen.

00:56:40.440 --> 00:56:42.820
And then you got to go to the left cause the rights blocked off.

00:56:42.960 --> 00:56:47.700
It doesn't happen. Instead, there's a lot of different, um, paths you can go on.

00:56:47.700 --> 00:56:56.240
And yeah, like some of them are just going to take you into, you know, not dead ends necessarily, but longer paths or more enemies or something like that.

00:56:56.400 --> 00:57:05.980
But that, that actually incentivizes for me a reason to play without a map because you might still get to the end, but it's just the path you choose of getting there.

00:57:05.980 --> 00:57:22.260
I thought that was kind of, um, an inspired choice to have a way to deal with the dungeons, have different experiences in the dungeons, but not necessarily change like the objective or make the objective puzzle-like in any particular way.

00:57:22.260 --> 00:57:27.980
It just makes it so that the route that, you know, you and I get there might be a little bit different.

00:57:28.200 --> 00:57:32.580
Yeah. And you might discover other things on a different route too, which I, which I found interesting.

00:57:33.420 --> 00:57:48.080
Um, I do want to also say though, in fairness, that, that there were a couple, there were two times where there were branching paths that I felt like, um, would have made a little bit more of a negative experience.

00:57:48.260 --> 00:57:50.200
So one was that it was actually a dungeon.

00:57:50.200 --> 00:57:51.960
I was spending a lot of time in.

00:57:52.000 --> 00:57:53.280
I wasn't sure. I didn't look at a guide.

00:57:53.280 --> 00:57:56.740
I wasn't sure if, um, if, uh, I was supposed to be there.

00:57:56.860 --> 00:57:58.020
I assumed it was where I was supposed to be.

00:57:58.180 --> 00:58:02.560
Finally, I was like, I was at what, you know, those bosses, those winged bosses that shoot fireballs.

00:58:02.580 --> 00:58:03.500
Yeah. It wasn't the worst thing.

00:58:03.640 --> 00:58:05.300
I was fighting one of them and I couldn't beat it.

00:58:05.360 --> 00:58:07.480
And I decided to look up a guide to make sure I was going the right direction.

00:58:08.300 --> 00:58:13.200
And when the first sentence in this section of the guide was like, this tower is entirely optional.

00:58:13.580 --> 00:58:15.020
So first I was like, okay, great.

00:58:15.360 --> 00:58:19.300
Uh, but it said like at the end of the tower, there's like a, there's a reward of like this.

00:58:19.460 --> 00:58:23.740
I don't know if it was a glove or it was something that you get it and it permanently increases your power.

00:58:23.840 --> 00:58:25.120
And I was like, okay, well that's definitely worth it.

00:58:25.120 --> 00:58:27.580
So I'm glad I put all this time in right below that.

00:58:27.580 --> 00:58:32.720
And I was like, I'm glad in the guide. It says, however, there's a bug in the game where at the start of the game, it thinks you already have this.

00:58:32.820 --> 00:58:34.520
So when you get it, it just takes it away.

00:58:35.100 --> 00:58:36.360
Whoa. Really?

00:58:36.720 --> 00:58:37.860
You're talking about the pendant.

00:58:38.760 --> 00:58:39.580
Yeah. The pendant.

00:58:39.780 --> 00:58:43.400
It was like, go ahead and continue on if you want to give yourself more of a challenge for the rest of the game.

00:58:43.660 --> 00:58:44.580
And I was like, you know what?

00:58:44.620 --> 00:58:45.880
I'm going to leave this tower.

00:58:46.280 --> 00:58:53.320
There's also another optional tower later on that's literally called the useless tower, which I didn't go into.

00:58:53.600 --> 00:58:58.500
But so like, you know, maybe, you know, there could have been some learnings for that.

00:58:58.560 --> 00:59:00.180
The first one is a bug problem.

00:59:00.320 --> 00:59:02.080
So if they fix the bug, it actually would have been cool.

00:59:02.080 --> 00:59:05.060
So two thoughts on that. So Joe, first thing, the useless tower, right?

00:59:05.060 --> 00:59:11.060
Can anything truly be useless in this game when there's a chance to earn experience points and level up?

00:59:11.880 --> 00:59:19.320
Of course, I would not even agree with that because I don't think that's true of anything where it's like, oh, well, you've been given the gift of grinding.

00:59:19.660 --> 00:59:22.300
Like I don't agree with that.

00:59:22.440 --> 00:59:29.880
But then the second thought, I'm surprised you brought up dependent because I had like a whole tangent that I was going to save for later on about that.

00:59:30.020 --> 00:59:33.860
But the dependent for me, and it is true.

00:59:33.860 --> 00:59:38.020
Basically, instead of making you like the whole game, you're overpowered.

00:59:38.100 --> 00:59:42.460
Right. Instead of making you overpowered, you actually get halved in power.

00:59:42.460 --> 00:59:44.580
So it's actually like a challenge mode thing.

00:59:44.580 --> 00:59:45.920
But it's a bugging error.

00:59:46.060 --> 00:59:47.180
It was not like a trick.

00:59:47.400 --> 00:59:49.080
They weren't intending to do that.

00:59:49.220 --> 00:59:50.880
It's it's definitely a bugging error.

00:59:50.880 --> 00:59:59.260
And that makes me just think about like there is such a case today, especially for those of you who are listening and replaying these games.

00:59:59.260 --> 01:00:08.480
Yes, there is some satisfaction in like playing the game exactly as it was intended on NES hardware with the original cartridge and everything like that.

01:00:08.480 --> 01:00:16.820
But I also think now is a better time than ever to look at the ROM hacking scene and really like see what improvement hacks they have.

01:00:16.900 --> 01:00:23.900
I'm not talking about people who like they take they make like Fizanadu 2 and they create their own crazy levels and stuff like that.

01:00:24.040 --> 01:00:31.740
Like, you know. But just a version of the game where you start with like normal strength and then dependent does the correct thing.

01:00:33.160 --> 01:00:34.060
Yeah. Wow.

01:00:34.840 --> 01:00:40.560
Right. Exactly. And that exists, you know, and there are plenty of hacks like that for all different kinds of games.

01:00:40.720 --> 01:00:51.320
There are hacks even for, you know, Final Fantasy 1 and Dragon Warrior where they actually just change the amount of experience points that things give.

01:00:51.420 --> 01:00:59.380
So they're not just like as easy as saying everything's doubled, but like thinking about, well, you know, these enemies are a little tougher, so they should give more experience points than they are giving.

01:00:59.380 --> 01:01:02.480
And I just still I can't.

01:01:02.580 --> 01:01:13.520
This is almost an unforgivable thing that they just like leave in the game that like we're talking about how how like forgiving the game is and how easy it is in some ways.

01:01:14.060 --> 01:01:20.160
And to think that this is not the balance that they were that they were going for is insane.

01:01:20.540 --> 01:01:27.800
Right. And also, I think that like because we were saying pretty positive things about like the challenge level of this game.

01:01:27.800 --> 01:01:29.980
And I think that they maybe accidentally.

01:01:30.200 --> 01:01:31.680
Yeah. Improved. Yeah.

01:01:32.000 --> 01:01:34.700
Like I think the bug actually sort of improved the game.

01:01:34.900 --> 01:01:42.440
I mean, I don't know how much of a difference it makes having this thing to not because I feel like still doesn't feel like I do a ton of damage in this game.

01:01:42.540 --> 01:01:51.040
I mean, so some of these enemies with depending which weapon you have still take like seven hits a little later in the game to just kill one regular enemy.

01:01:52.160 --> 01:01:59.180
So like, yeah, maybe they were intending it to be a little more Nintendo hard and they accidentally made it a little more accessible.

01:02:00.240 --> 01:02:02.500
Yeah, that's which is which is crazy.

01:02:02.680 --> 01:02:09.880
I don't know what it says about the about their their design for this game, but the final product worked out.

01:02:09.880 --> 01:02:12.320
I think it's also because dependent is optional.

01:02:12.540 --> 01:02:18.440
Right. If it was something where like you, you know, you need this to go to the final castle.

01:02:18.640 --> 01:02:22.160
Right. Then it's like, OK, that's a it's a little more strange.

01:02:22.280 --> 01:02:24.040
Right. Because then it's almost like how did they.

01:02:24.960 --> 01:02:26.700
Yeah. Yeah, exactly.

01:02:26.700 --> 01:02:32.880
But it's also weird in a in a game with, you know, we're not we're not pretending like there's optional towers galore.

01:02:33.040 --> 01:02:36.520
Right. There was only like three or four optional side quests in the whole game.

01:02:36.660 --> 01:02:41.140
And so to make like to not test those things either, it feels a little strange, obviously.

01:02:41.280 --> 01:02:47.800
But again, that's just my case for people to a reason to check out ROM hacks on top of your traditional playing.

01:02:47.800 --> 01:02:53.560
We know some of these games are like, you know, just cozy to play the way that they were originally made and stuff like that.

01:02:53.560 --> 01:03:05.700
But I also think there is a case to, you know, to find balance in modern game design being reapplied to these older games that, you know, not necessarily the designers were thinking about at the time.

01:03:05.900 --> 01:03:07.780
Yeah. I mean, even if it's just quality of life stuff.

01:03:07.920 --> 01:03:17.960
I mean, I will say I was very annoyed every time I wanted to buy five potions and I had to reopen the shop menu for each potion and like goes with the whole conversation for each potion.

01:03:18.040 --> 01:03:19.980
I can't just say I'd like five potions.

01:03:21.520 --> 01:03:22.480
Yeah, that's true.

01:03:24.020 --> 01:03:31.640
It was was Charles Barkley shut up and jam the original a ROM hack or was that never an actual game?

01:03:32.200 --> 01:03:34.900
Yeah, it's an actual game, but probably made with RPG.

01:03:35.100 --> 01:03:41.460
OK, I should look into that, but I definitely know it was made with some kind of RPG like engine.

01:03:41.680 --> 01:03:43.180
You know, I don't know if it was specifically.

01:03:43.360 --> 01:03:46.080
I know you owned a copy of RPG maker on PlayStation.

01:03:46.460 --> 01:03:47.380
I don't think they use that.

01:03:47.620 --> 01:03:49.180
Yeah. OK.

01:04:05.560 --> 01:04:11.300
We talked briefly about those gurus who do the they create the meditations for the save passwords.

01:04:11.660 --> 01:04:19.080
But I mean, like maybe it was supposed to be this way, but it is kind of funny that they're like, you know, sit down here and meditate with me.

01:04:19.080 --> 01:04:21.600
And then they just say like the most.

01:04:23.260 --> 01:04:24.140
Yeah, exactly.

01:04:24.340 --> 01:04:31.040
It's like just a pile of letter vomit on you that like I guess if you wanted to try it, you could be like, you know, it's.

01:04:33.800 --> 01:04:36.840
You know, like you could try and say like, oh, that's how he's saying it.

01:04:36.940 --> 01:04:51.340
Right. But more than likely, he's just saying ridiculous letters that correspond to passwords that mean something very specific for the exact loadout that you have based on experience points, gold, armor, progress that you've made.

01:04:51.520 --> 01:04:53.460
I don't really have a problem with it.

01:04:53.600 --> 01:04:59.440
I mean, it's just a way to build the passwords into the lore of the game.

01:04:59.440 --> 01:05:01.880
No, absolutely. I'm saying I think it's actually kind of neat, right?

01:05:02.020 --> 01:05:04.700
It's like they're up with their own language there.

01:05:04.780 --> 01:05:10.960
But when I was looking up on some background in this game, everyone apparently hates it and just makes fun of it at all times.

01:05:12.200 --> 01:05:20.540
I was surprised because, yeah, I also think that, like, is this the first time we've seen them try to contextualize it, contextualize a password in the world?

01:05:20.600 --> 01:05:23.660
Like, it feels like it really just never wants to take you out of the world.

01:05:24.180 --> 01:05:25.040
Yeah, I don't know.

01:05:25.180 --> 01:05:28.940
I think the only other time that I can think of, like, no, that wasn't even passwords.

01:05:28.940 --> 01:05:30.220
It was like a save feature.

01:05:31.320 --> 01:05:32.400
So, yeah, I don't know.

01:05:32.420 --> 01:05:35.460
I think this might be the only time we've seen this.

01:05:35.920 --> 01:05:40.880
Yeah, I mean, I guess, OK, just a couple weeks ago with Dragon Warrior Quest.

01:05:40.880 --> 01:05:47.380
Well, no, not no, because they would they would save your you know, you would take a rest and they would save your file.

01:05:47.460 --> 01:05:48.980
So I think this is the first time we're seeing passwords.

01:05:48.980 --> 01:05:56.700
Passwords, passwords specifically, but but at least the save file and that was like, oh, if you want, go to the king and he'll he'll like write down.

01:05:57.000 --> 01:06:00.140
Oh, yeah. He'll write down your in the journal or whatever.

01:06:00.400 --> 01:06:02.340
He's taking an audit of your.

01:06:02.460 --> 01:06:03.620
Yeah. Yeah.

01:06:04.080 --> 01:06:13.860
So like one other thing that about this game that I just wanted to mention is going back to what I said at the beginning about the presentation.

01:06:13.860 --> 01:06:24.280
Like this game between it's like very restrained, like color palette and very detailed backgrounds.

01:06:24.280 --> 01:06:31.520
And it's just a very pretty game, like especially like the the missed levels.

01:06:31.520 --> 01:06:37.600
Like I didn't even think that the NES could could do like the kind of the kind of effects it was doing.

01:06:37.760 --> 01:06:51.340
But regardless of if it's like just that, yeah, that missed or just the details of the wood when you're in the tree or just the darkness of the palette and like the enemy design.

01:06:51.340 --> 01:07:02.640
Like it all like really feels like the same thing, whereas I think a lot of games, RPGs included, just sort of feels like this mishmash of assets that get thrown together.

01:07:02.880 --> 01:07:08.740
Like I think that the holisticness of the style in this game is worth mentioning.

01:07:10.280 --> 01:07:21.720
Yeah. And a lot of I feel like a lot of times I notice when a when a game is really bright and colorful, like maybe like the original Legend of Zelda or Zelda 2 or whatever, like they look cleaner.

01:07:22.080 --> 01:07:28.460
And then when they're trying to make it look a little darker, I don't want to say gritty, but they want to try and make it look a little more earthy.

01:07:28.620 --> 01:07:35.240
And then when they try to make it look a little more like have that little more like realism to the color palette that like on the NES would always get a little messier.

01:07:35.240 --> 01:07:38.120
And I was impressed that they kind of they kind of married both of those.

01:07:38.240 --> 01:07:44.920
And like this is like there are a lot of times where things are dimmer, things are a little a little darker and it still feels very clean.

01:07:45.080 --> 01:07:47.540
Granted, there were a couple of times where I couldn't see a door that was right.

01:07:47.960 --> 01:07:49.360
Like blended in with the background.

01:07:49.600 --> 01:07:56.960
But those those times were were few and far between compared to how how nice it felt.

01:07:57.140 --> 01:08:02.680
And it also just like I really want to just hit home how each area feels.

01:08:03.940 --> 01:08:12.340
One, like its own area and to like a very natural part of this world that they made based on the visuals, you know.

01:08:12.520 --> 01:08:23.180
So there's the there's the I think it's right out of the town of like four paw or one of the third town is where you have to do this little these those side quests.

01:08:23.300 --> 01:08:25.880
We talk about where you got to find these fountains and that's just like one.

01:08:25.880 --> 01:08:29.700
And that's just like this one town that's relying on these fountains and you're helping them.

01:08:29.760 --> 01:08:32.940
It's a nice chunk of the game where you feel like I'm in this area.

01:08:33.100 --> 01:08:36.100
Like, you know, I'm in this I'm in this land right now.

01:08:36.100 --> 01:08:38.800
This part of the tree where these people live and they need this.

01:08:38.860 --> 01:08:42.500
And I'm doing this whole quest going all over, you know, this area.

01:08:42.620 --> 01:08:45.260
And then later on, I think it's the town of Victim.

01:08:46.260 --> 01:08:47.800
Everything has this like mist.

01:08:48.040 --> 01:08:49.160
What a great town. Yeah.

01:08:49.320 --> 01:08:51.080
Victim. Some of these names are the town.

01:08:51.780 --> 01:08:56.400
But the town of Victim, everything has this like mist overlaid over it.

01:08:56.540 --> 01:08:58.440
And everything feels very eerie.

01:08:58.600 --> 01:09:04.820
And it has this very I'm a sucker for like well done tones in retro games.

01:09:04.940 --> 01:09:07.140
And this has this very eerie tone.

01:09:07.140 --> 01:09:13.860
And it ties into the story that like this is where the meteor hit and this in the tree in this area is burning.

01:09:13.860 --> 01:09:16.680
So there's all this mist and smoke that's kind of around.

01:09:16.680 --> 01:09:19.380
And it feels very haunted and creepy.

01:09:19.680 --> 01:09:22.220
And every area kind of feels like its own area.

01:09:22.220 --> 01:09:25.060
And it really does a good job of setting a mood.

01:09:25.260 --> 01:09:26.680
And the characters too.

