01:16:20.200 --> 01:16:21.340
The opinion remains.

01:16:21.760 --> 01:16:29.720
That this game between like the presentational, stylistic things that this game does well.

01:16:30.180 --> 01:16:32.680
It's just a prettier to look at game for me.

01:16:36.660 --> 01:16:40.620
The coherence of its plot while it's still simple.

01:16:40.780 --> 01:16:45.400
The fact that it's all happening in this sort of well-realized world.

01:16:46.520 --> 01:16:47.960
It's got its flaws.

01:16:48.320 --> 01:16:50.540
I still think the combat's a little sticky.

01:16:50.740 --> 01:16:54.720
But then I kind of think that about all the games in this micro-genre.

01:16:57.080 --> 01:16:59.860
I think that the experience was good enough for me.

01:17:00.020 --> 01:17:04.880
And this was a surprising enough experience that I would put this on the Essential Games list.

01:17:05.220 --> 01:17:06.840
So is that a yes?

01:17:07.240 --> 01:17:08.420
I would.

01:17:08.720 --> 01:17:10.480
He's not. It's a yes.

01:17:11.800 --> 01:17:13.820
There we go. So Sean has put it on.

01:17:14.540 --> 01:17:18.080
It is now time for Joe to vote.

01:17:19.580 --> 01:17:21.920
Well, I think it's actually a little ironic.

01:17:21.920 --> 01:17:25.180
Because a couple weeks ago we played Dragon Quest.

01:17:25.920 --> 01:17:28.460
That was like one of the first games that was really about...

01:17:28.460 --> 01:17:32.060
I was like, yes, this is about to scratch that itch that I've been like waiting for.

01:17:32.160 --> 01:17:32.980
I mean, we've had other ones yet.

01:17:33.020 --> 01:17:35.340
But this is like what I've been waiting for.

01:17:35.440 --> 01:17:37.020
It's like this JRPG style.

01:17:38.260 --> 01:17:39.120
And it did.

01:17:39.240 --> 01:17:40.980
It was good. It wasn't great.

01:17:41.140 --> 01:17:42.080
You know, it had its problems.

01:17:42.200 --> 01:17:45.280
It was clearly like, okay, this is still very early in the JRPG thing.

01:17:47.260 --> 01:17:52.500
Enter Faxanadu, which doesn't do any of the things that I was attributing to that feeling that I wanted.

01:17:52.760 --> 01:17:56.240
It's not an overworld, like open, you go in any direction.

01:17:56.440 --> 01:18:02.220
It's not a turn-based RPG where you have time to think about your strategy and stuff like that.

01:18:03.520 --> 01:18:08.040
But somehow it scratches that itch more than Dragon Warrior does.

01:18:08.060 --> 01:18:10.480
I'm just going to keep bouncing back and forth between Dragon Quest and Dragon Warrior.

01:18:11.960 --> 01:18:18.620
And I feel like it just has like the soul of that going on an adventure journey.

01:18:19.040 --> 01:18:26.660
And one of the better, like more robust and more well-realized storytelling or just stories in general.

01:18:26.780 --> 01:18:34.740
I just can't get over how much I feel like this is one cohesive whole experience where it's like you're on the world tree.

01:18:36.860 --> 01:18:42.820
And the story feels like it's not super complicated, like you said, but it feels like there's weight to it.

01:18:42.900 --> 01:18:43.920
And I care about it.

01:18:43.960 --> 01:18:45.500
I'm interested a little bit in the story.

01:18:45.740 --> 01:18:59.060
And it's one of those games, maybe one of the most that we've played, that is making me feel like I feel with a modern game that I've chosen to play outside of this podcast that I just get really into.

01:18:59.060 --> 01:19:04.580
You know, it's like if I pick up The Witcher and I get really into The Witcher and now like every day I'm excited to play a little more of that.

01:19:04.780 --> 01:19:05.720
That's how I feel with this.

01:19:05.760 --> 01:19:06.660
Like I said, I didn't beat it.

01:19:06.740 --> 01:19:08.320
I got moderately close.

01:19:08.500 --> 01:19:11.260
I'm excited to pick it back up again and keep playing.

01:19:12.160 --> 01:19:15.940
So if I'm not telegraphing this enough, I'm definitely going to vote this essential.

01:19:16.860 --> 01:19:19.020
And it could be recency bias.

01:19:19.820 --> 01:19:32.640
So I won't make this statement fully, but like I'll be interested to see after some time goes by exactly where I want to place this on my personal like favorite games of 1989 or favorite games of the NES so far.

01:19:33.760 --> 01:19:36.960
So, yeah, I'm definitely voting for this to be essential game.

01:19:37.260 --> 01:19:41.320
All right. And as you guys know, there's two votes for the essential games list.

01:19:41.420 --> 01:19:43.580
So it is automatically on the essential games list.

01:19:43.740 --> 01:19:47.080
It's our newest game on the essential games list.

01:19:47.220 --> 01:19:48.680
And I can't do anything about it.

01:19:48.680 --> 01:19:50.000
But I still get to vote.

01:19:50.180 --> 01:19:51.800
And so I will vote right now.

01:19:52.220 --> 01:19:55.240
As you may have, I mean, some of you might have even counted.

01:19:55.960 --> 01:20:02.400
I have said Zelda 2's name a lot on this podcast, but I've mentioned some other games as well.

01:20:02.580 --> 01:20:07.700
But, you know, none of these games can be compared to each other because they are all offering something different.

01:20:07.880 --> 01:20:17.420
My point for bringing up Zelda 2 is because I feel like more than ever the heat on, you know, Zelda 2 not being an essential game is pretty high.

01:20:17.420 --> 01:20:32.480
And so when I'm looking at a game like Phyzanadu here that, you know, finds some similar things, I found that almost every complaint for things I had in Zelda 2 was answered by a game like Phyzanadu here.

01:20:32.480 --> 01:20:38.640
And what I mean by that is this game is still not like a perfect game by any means.

01:20:38.640 --> 01:20:42.020
And it doesn't offer the same experience that Zelda 2 does.

01:20:42.160 --> 01:20:53.300
But I find very little to complain about the actual, you know, story, gameplay, dungeon, progression aspects of this game.

01:20:53.300 --> 01:21:00.100
There are things that I personally don't like about them or things that I was like, oh, well, you know, that's a little tedious.

01:21:00.800 --> 01:21:06.480
I definitely can see that with the combat, you know, since it is just the same kind of fights over and over again.

01:21:06.620 --> 01:21:09.460
I think they did a good job of doing what they could, right?

01:21:09.520 --> 01:21:11.820
They weren't going to completely change the combat system on its face.

01:21:11.900 --> 01:21:15.540
But because it's just something that happens so often, it is what it is at this point.

01:21:15.540 --> 01:21:28.020
Overall, though, this game for me not only exceeded my expectations, but because of how many people were making comparisons to Zelda 2 leading up to this episode,

01:21:28.220 --> 01:21:37.180
I went in with that mindset of like, all right, well, you know, if Zelda 2 doesn't make the essential games list, why does Phyzanadu if I'm going to vote for it?

01:21:37.180 --> 01:21:46.940
And I can confidently say that I don't even need to defend Phyzanadu against Zelda 2 or against any other game on the essential games list because it kind of just stands on its own.

01:21:47.160 --> 01:21:56.980
Like Sean and Joe were saying, this is really a game that doesn't need to be compared to others because it's just a good, tight experience that isn't necessarily NES hard,

01:21:56.980 --> 01:22:06.360
but still feels like you're making progress and being part of a larger world, stuff that we're just not used to seeing as fully on the NES.

01:22:06.360 --> 01:22:10.920
So I really liked Phyzanadu and yeah, I agree with Joe.

01:22:11.000 --> 01:22:18.940
This is a game that I was excited to be playing and I probably will be excited to continue playing it as I get closer to the end of my journey.

